course: dictionary
lesson: 12671
title: Distinct: clear and different
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Distinct» means clearly different, or easy to see, hear, smell or notice. Think of it as clear and separate. The words around it give you clues.
explain: «The two groups sat in distinct corners of the hall.» The groups were far apart and easy to tell apart, so distinct means clearly separate. Try your meaning in the sentence: "clearly separate corners" still makes sense.
explain: «There was a distinct smell of toast in the kitchen.» You could clearly notice the smell. Distinct does not mean the smell was big or loud. It means it was clear enough to notice.
fact: «Distinct» means clearly different or separate from something else.
fact: «Distinct» can also mean easy to see, hear, smell or notice.
fact: «Distinct» can mean definite or real, as in «a distinct chance of rain».
fact: «Distinct from» means different from something else.
fact: In maths, «three distinct numbers» means no two of the numbers are the same.
exercise: «Sort the animals into distinct groups.» What does distinct mean here? | Hidden away | Very big | Easy to hear | *Clearly different from each other
exercise: «There was a distinct smell of smoke as we walked into the kitchen.» What does distinct mean here? | Quite cold | Very old | Hard to find | *Easy to notice
exercise: Which word means clear and easy to notice? | dim | *distinct | blurred
test: «There is a distinct chance of rain today.» What does distinct mean here? | Silly and funny | Very wet and grey | Far away | *Definite and real, not just a guess
test: «Her answer was distinct from mine.» What does «distinct from» mean? | Close to | *Different from | Far away from | Looking the same as
exam: Which sentence uses distinct to mean easy to notice? | The two shapes are distinct | Distinct is a long word | *A distinct bang came from the kitchen | Distinct means old
